eyes on exec V 2.0
==================

Installation
------------

1. Create a character device with major 127 and minor 0
   (maybe via mknod /dev/exec c 127 0)
   with name /dev/exec
     
2. compile the module: 'cc -c -Wall -O2 eoe20.c'
   for 2.0 kernels or
   'cc -c -O2 eoe22.c' for 2.2 kernels.
       
3. insmod it: 'insmod eoe'

4. If you want, compile the sample-program dr.c also
   and run it. You should know see which programs are
   executed.

If you have trouble with the major-number of 127, you may use
a own by #defining another EXEC_MAJOR in eoe.c.
If you have other (non-trivial) trouble, you may drop me a line.   
--   

Eyes on exec is a small auditing system wich creates a device
/dev/exec. If you do a 
	. open() 

and then a 
	. read() 

over it, you will get the programs wich were recently executed by 
the user with EUID = 0. The read-call is a slow system call, so it
can block until the next program is executed. If you open /dev/exec
with the O_NONBLOCK flag, you can poll this device.
If you call 
	. ioctl() with cmd = EOE_SET{EUID,ALL}

you can set the EUID you want to monitor. You can't 
	. write() 

to this device (i hope you understand this :-).
Also a 
	. lseek()

is not implemented (why?)
A 
	. select()

should be implemented in the next versions.
You should 
	. close()

this device if you don't need it longer. This will only decrease
the MOD_COUNT, so if this reaches 0 you can rmmod it. BTW,
open() increases it.
All the operations can only be called by the superuser.
Feel free to contact me if you have comments/questions.
You can get the latest version of EoE at
http://www.cs.uni-potsdam.de/homepages/students/linuxer

At this time, EoE is licensed under the GPL. So you use it at your own
risk. To get a copy of the GPL-license, download the 'logit' package from
the homepage above which includes the file COYPING.

There are still some minor bugs in eoe20 if you do
execve(NULL,NULL,NULL); will this cause the kernel to dump
registers. This is fixed in eoe22.c.
I'm also not sure if the signal-handling in eoe22.c is correct,
so i would claim eoe22.c to be BETA.

S. Krahmer	<krahmer@cs.uni-potsdam.de>

